          "type": "turn",
          "question": "my face on one sied went funny this morning and my speach came out wrong for about ten minits, its ok now",
          "answer": "**Call emergency services now.** Facial drooping on one side and disturbed speech are recognised warning signs of a stroke or transient ischaemic attack (TIA, a brief stroke-like episode). They need same-day emergency evaluation even though they have gone away. Do not wait to see if they come back. (Source: red-flag-stroke-symptoms.)\n\nWhile you wait for help or travel to care:\n\n- **Write down the time** the symptoms started, and the time they stopped. If you do not know exactly when they started, note the last time you were known to be well.\n- **Tell the person you are with** what happened, so they can tell the responders too.\n- **Do not drive yourself.**\n\nReport these to the emergency team even if they seem to have settled:\n- Weakness or numbness in one arm, or one arm drifting down when both are raised\n- Numbness or weakness in a leg, or one whole side of the body\n- Slurred speech, trouble finding words, or trouble understanding others\n- Sudden confusion\n- Sudden loss or blurring of vision in one or both eyes\n- Sudden difficulty walking, dizziness, or loss of balance or coordination\n- A sudden severe headache with no known cause\n\n(Source: red-flag-stroke-symptoms.)\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
